{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, because you heed.</b> Rashi explained: commandments that a person treads with his heels — i.e., light commandments. And this is what David said: 'Why should I fear in the days of evil, the iniquity of my heels shall surround me.' David said: Of the stringent commandments in the Torah I am not afraid, for I have fulfilled them. But of the light commandments that a person treads with his heels — I am afraid, lest because it is light in my eyes I did not take care to fulfill it. And you said: 'Be careful in a light commandment as in a stringent one.' And likewise it is written: 'The path of life — lest you weigh the paths of her feet, you will not know' — i.e., lest you weigh the commandments whose reward is great and do them and neglect the light ones; therefore the paths of Torah are unstable, and you will not know the giving of their reward. A parable: to a king who had an orchard and brought laborers there to plant it, and did not reveal to them the reward for planting, and for which planting he would give abundant reward — for had he revealed it to them, all would run to the plantings whose reward is great and plant them, and the work of the orchard would be partly maintained and partly neglected. So too the commandments — had the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed their reward, some of them would be neglected. And Rabbi Shimon ben Yochai taught: two commandments the Holy One, blessed be He, revealed their reward — the lightest of the light and the most stringent of the stringent: sending the mother bird and honoring father and mother, and both of them their reward is the same: length of days in both — to show the world that there is a commandment that appears light in a person's eyes yet its reward is great. And this is 'and it shall be, because you heed' — that even the light commandments you will heed and observe and do them. And so David said: 'In keeping them, great reward.' And it is written: 'How great is Your goodness, which You have hidden for those who fear You.' And yet there are those who wonder: are there really commandments that a person treads with his heels? One may answer: for example, sending the mother bird, for it is written 'if a bird's nest happens before you,' and we derive: excluding one that is prepared — so when he walks and treads with his heels upon the nest. Alternatively, the commandment of tzitzit, which a person drags after him according to the custom of the world. However, I have heard that whoever drags his tzitzit upon himself — the verse says: ״וטאטאתיה במטאטא השמד״ (sweep it with the broom of destruction). Rather, one casts them over the shoulder and holds them in the hand."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :daat-zkenim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:00:41.311752144Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}